{"name":"Magical Merlin","hex":"#3d8ed0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"magical","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or by means of magic.","Enchanting."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magical"},{"term":"merlin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small falcon, Falco columbarius, that breeds in northern North America, Europe, and Asia."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cytoskeletal protein active in the suppression of tumors."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/merlin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Merlin","blocks":[{"text":"Merlin is a mythical figure prominently featured in the legend of King Arthur and best known as a prophet and a magician, along with several other main roles. The familiar depiction of Merlin, based on an amalgamation of historical and legendary figures, was introduced by the 12th-century Catholic cleric Geoffrey of Monmouth and then built on by the French poet Robert de Boron and prose successors in the 13th century. Geoffrey's account presented Merlin as a prophet and royal advisor to Arthur's father, Uther Pendragon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Arthur-Pyle_The_Enchanter_Merlin.JPG/330px-Arthur-Pyle_The_Enchanter_Merlin.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merlin","wikidata":"Q188044"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Nuremberg_chronicles_f_138r_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Nuremberg_chronicles_f_138r_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANuremberg_chronicles_f_138r_5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Blackbird_%28Turdus_merula%29_%2811%2